{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"Strategy: treat the conjunction independently.\n\nFor the first conjunct, use polarization. The quadratic map associated to \\(B\\) is \\(Q(z)=B(z,z)\\), so\n\\[\nQ(x+y)-Q(x)-Q(y)=B(x,y)+B(y,x).\n\\]\nThe associated bilinear map is one half of this polar form, giving exactly the stated action of the inverse of \\(2\\). This is the content of `QuadraticMap.associated_toQuadraticMap`. [Mathlib documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/QuadraticForm/Basic.html#QuadraticMap.associated_toQuadraticMap)\n\nFor the second conjunct, invoke the uniqueness mechanism for reflections preserving a finite spanning set, encapsulated by `Module.Dual.eq_of_preReflection_mapsTo`. Conceptually, let \\(r_f,r_g\\) be the two reflections and set \\(u=r_g r_f\\). Since both preserve the finite spanning set \\(\\Phi\\), \\(u\\) has finite order. Direct calculation gives\n\\[\nu=\\mathrm{id}+(f-g)\\otimes x,\n\\qquad ((f-g)\\otimes x)^2=0,\n\\]\nbecause \\(f(x)=g(x)=2\\). Hence \\(u^n=\\mathrm{id}+n((f-g)\\otimes x)\\). Finite order forces the rank-one term to vanish; characteristic zero and torsion-freeness allow cancellation of \\(n\\), while \\(f(x)=2\\) ensures \\(x\\neq0\\). Thus \\(f=g\\). Finiteness and spanning are the essential hypotheses turning preservation of \\(\\Phi\\) into finite order. [Mathlib documentation](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/Reflection.html)"}}
